# Patch 0083 — Personal knowledge graph + about:knowledge (ROADMAP Phase 7;
# FEATURES 6.3; ADR 0016). The north-star line this closes: "the browser
# understands relationships between papers, sites, notes, conversations".
#
# WHAT IS NEW IS ONE FACT, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE NO OTHER STORE HAS. Places
# records that you visited page A and page B. It does not record that A led to
# B. That single edge — plus "B was opened in a new tab FROM A" — is the
# research trail, and it is all this patch materializes:
#
#   edges     — followed / opened-from, deduplicated into one row with a
#               weight, so "I keep coming back here from there" is visible.
#   entities  — names a local model pulled out of a page, ON DEMAND ONLY.
#
# EVERYTHING ELSE IS DERIVED AT QUERY TIME from stores that already exist: the
# personal index (ADR 0012) for page text and term overlap, the knowledge
# store (ADR 0015) for notes/highlights/clips, the Places attribution table
# (ADR 0005) for which Space a page belongs to. Materializing those as edges
# would create a second copy that can disagree with the first, and a graph
# that disagrees with your notes reads as the browser being wrong about your
# own work.
#
# DELETION FOLLOWS PLACES HERE — the opposite of the store it sits next to,
# and deliberately. An edge records WHERE YOU WENT, so clearing history must
# clear it; a note records what you WROTE, so clearing history must not. One
# SQLite file cannot hold two deletion contracts, which is the whole reason
# kavacha-graph.sqlite is separate from kavacha-knowledge.sqlite.
#
# NO EDGE IS GUESSED. `opened-from` is written only when tabbrowser actually
# set `owner` on the new tab; if it did not, nothing is recorded rather than
# inferring "whatever was selected at the time". A wrong edge is worse than a
# missing one, because the graph is read as a record of what happened. Leaving
# the web (about:, file:) forgets the trail instead of stitching the next page
# onto the last one across it, and a self-edge is a reload, not a relationship.
#
# NO BACKGROUND MODEL CALLS. Entity extraction runs when the user presses
# "Find names on this page" — never on load, never on a timer. Patch 0079's
# guarantee (a fresh profile makes zero AI requests until a feature is
# invoked; SHIPPING R3) therefore survives this patch, and the reply is parsed
# as a hostile string exactly like patch 0081's tab grouping: find the array,
# keep entries with a plausible name, drop everything else. A confused model
# leaves a page un-enriched; it cannot write anything else into the store.
#
# THE PAGE IS NOT A FORCE-DIRECTED BALL OF DOTS, on purpose. A spring layout
# over a few thousand pages is a screensaver: it looks like understanding and
# answers no question. about:knowledge answers the three questions a person
# actually asks of a page — what led me here, what did I write about it, what
# else is like it — and the module API carries no presentation, so a different
# view can be built on the same data later.
#
# SIMILARITY IS LEXICAL, and the patch says so where a user can see it: it
# finds the paper that also says "flood mapping" and misses the one that says
# "inundation modelling". ADR 0012's optional local embeddings are what would
# close that gap, and this is the first caller that would benefit.
#
